<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cantar de mio Cid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1200</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Poema de mio Cid, Poem of the Cid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cantar de mio Cid (Poem of the Cid) is the oldest surviving epic poem in Castilian Spanish, telling of the exile and triumphs of Rodrigo Díaz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a>. Composed around 1200, it survives in a single 14th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> copied from a text signed by a certain Per Abbat. It recounts the Cid's conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and the restoration of his honour, and is a founding work of Spanish literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cantar-de-mio-cid.jpg" alt="Cantar de mio Cid" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Per Abbat (copista) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cantar_de_mio_Cid_f._1r_(rep).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The poem is anonymous and survives in a 14th-century codex in the Spanish National Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, which lacks its first page and two others in the middle. A colophon names Per Abbad, in English Abbot Peter, and puts the date 1207 after his name. Current thinking is that this claim was simply copied with the text of an earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> now lost. Ramón Menéndez Pidal counted the work among the poems passed down and varied by minstrels, while others believe a single learned author wrote it. Date and authorship remain open to debate.</p><p>The original title is unknown, and the present one was proposed by Menéndez Pidal. Cid comes from the Arabic sidi or sayyid, a title like Sir. The poem has been translated into English many times since 1879, among others by John Ormsby, W. S. Merwin and Burton Raffel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Since 1913 the poem, of more than 3,700 verses, has been divided into three parts. In the first, enemies accuse Rodrigo Díaz of keeping tribute owed to King Alfonso VI, who exiles him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León. He leaves his wife, Doña Ximena, and his daughters, Doña Elvira and Doña Sol, at the monastery of Cardeña and grows rich from raids on Moorish territory. In the second he takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and defeats the Almoravid king Chufa ibn Tashfin, and the king pardons him and allows his daughters to marry the infantes of Carrión.</p><p>In the third part the infantes show cowardice, fleeing a lion in the court and a campaign against the Moors. In revenge they beat their wives and leave them tied to trees at Corpes. The Cid asks the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> for justice, his men defeat the infantes in a duel, and his daughters marry the heirs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and Aragon. The tone is realist, with no magic: the archangel Gabriel appears only in a dream.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The poem is the oldest preserved Castilian epic and is considered a national epic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. It departs from history: it does not mention the Cid's son, and his daughters were not called Elvira and Sol. It echoes the plot devices of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Roland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cantar de mio Cid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The career of El Cid inspired the <b>Cantar de mio Cid</b>, probably composed around 1200, the earliest surviving epic in Castilian, a language that would later spread across Spain and the Americas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The story inspired the epic <b>Cantar de mio Cid</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-valencia">Lordship of Valencia</a></li><li><blockquote>It is known mainly from Christian sources and from the epic <b>Cantar de mio Cid</b>, in which its lord Abengalbón is a loyal ally who hosts the Cid's wife and daughters.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-molina">Taifa of Molina</a></li></ul></section>
